# The Pour V4 captive slide; reviewed after the published STEP and print-surface payload matched.
# Findings read once from the current STL. Physical release, strength and lifetime remain unmeasured.

[ceiling] This is the single moving crossbar underside at Z3.56. Its two bed-rooted legs anchor the 13 mm span along Y; the native first crossbar layer contains 37 Y-directed bridge roads with both leg anchors. The fixed plinth stops at Z3.08, leaving the intended nominal 0.48 mm release air. The 3.36 mm bar carries the local sliding reference.
alternatives: A solid bridge down to the plinth would join the moving and fixed bodies. Slicer support under this crossbar would fill the deliberate release gap; the bar instead bridges its own two printed legs. Shortening the span would change the retained two-leg portal and its accessible finger opening.
file: hardware/printed-parts/calibration/soda-studies/arch-moving.step
click: x=0.000 y=14.750 z=3.560

[thin] These four ranked regions are the moving legs' intentional first-layer relief. Each 3 mm leg retracts 0.35 mm per face at the bed and returns to its full section by Z1; its narrowest bed contact is 2.3 mm. That local exception increases first-layer separation from the fixed running faces and end stops. The native bed-layer model footprints are separate; ordinary leg stock above Z1 remains 3 mm.
alternatives: Removing the relief retains 3 mm at the bed but removes the additional first-layer air. A 3.7 mm leg with the same relief retains 3 mm bed contact but enlarges this two-leg portal by 1.4 mm along Y or shortens its 13 mm clear span. This short relieved foot is a fit reference, with no load or service-life qualification claimed.
